<!DOCTYPE qhelp PUBLIC
  "-//Semmle//qhelp//EN"
  "qhelp.dtd">
<qhelp>
<overview>
<p>
Authentication tokens such as signed JSON Web Tokens (JWTs), access tokens, refresh tokens and
session tokens are active credentials. Anyone who reads a log file that contains such a token
can replay it to impersonate the user or service it was issued to, until it expires or is revoked.
Logs are frequently copied to aggregation systems and are readable by a wider audience than the
application's secrets.
</p>
<p>
The standard <code>go/clear-text-logging</code> query only treats password-like values, HTTP request
headers and Kubernetes secrets as sensitive. This query additionally treats the result of signing a JWT
(for example <code>Token.SignedString</code> from <code>github.com/golang-jwt/jwt</code> or
<code>github.com/dgrijalva/jwt-go</code>) and string values whose names indicate a credential token
as sensitive.
</p>
</overview>

<recommendation>
<p>
Do not log authentication tokens. If a token must be correlated across log entries, log a
non-reversible identifier instead, such as the token's <code>jti</code> claim or a cryptographic hash of
the token.
</p>
</recommendation>

<example>
<p>
The following example generates a token at startup and writes it to the log:
</p>
<sample language="go">
token := jwt.NewWithClaims(jwt.SigningMethodHS256, claims)
signed, _ := token.SignedString(key)
log.Printf("issued token %s", signed) // BAD
</sample>
<p>
Instead, log information that does not disclose the credential:
</p>
<sample language="go">
token := jwt.NewWithClaims(jwt.SigningMethodHS256, claims)
signed, _ := token.SignedString(key)
log.Printf("issued token for %s", claims.Subject) // GOOD
</sample>
</example>

<references>
<li>OWASP: <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Logging_Cheat_Sheet.html">Logging Cheat Sheet</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/312.html">CWE-312</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/315.html">CWE-315</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/359.html">CWE-359</a>.</li>
<li>Common Weakness Enumeration: <a href="https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/532.html">CWE-532</a>.</li>
</references>
</qhelp>
